Bhupati patel is an old hand at this  he's been farming since he was a  teenager here in Eastern Mudder Pradesh  growing pulses and cereal crops but  these aren't Patel's fields today his  other friends are in the midst of the  wheat harvest and he's struggling with  painful memories in mid-march unseasonal  he'll hit the neighboring district with  Patel's own four acre farms located the  storm battered his crops and pushed him  to a mental breakdown the hailstorm  completely destroyed my chickpeas I had  borrowed money too so the crops my first  thought was how will I repay the loan I  was shocked I couldn't think straight I  swallowed some pesticide too in my life  Patel was rushed to hospital where he  spent days recovery he's still weak and  suffers from frequent dizziness and  nausea he's not sure when he can work  again at the beginning of the year Patel  took a bank loan of over 2,500 euros to  buy seeds and fertilizers rent a tractor  and hire a farmhand he also borrowed  from moneylenders to cover his family's  expenses the debt burden kept growing  our crops are a lifeline if they fail  where do we get money from I now have no  source of income for the rest of the  year I felt there was just no way out  for me I wanted to die lupard Patel is  not an isolated case and it's not  confined to states like multiple dish  across India hundreds of debt relief  farmers have committed suicide over the  years the agrarian crisis is a major  issue in the ongoing Indian election  with all political parties trying to woo  farmers  on the outskirts of Jabalpur the  opposition Congress party is holding a  campaign rally its leader Rahul Gandhi  is promising a separate budget for  farmers a complete loan waiver and his  bowing the criminal proceedings will not  be allowed against farmers who default  on their loans nearly 70% of India still  relies on agriculture for a living  experts say there's a lot of political  rhetoric around this huge voting bloc  but little will to tackle the underlying  crisis what India needs to do what  political parties need to do is to go  into the root of the agrarian crisis on  how farmers and farming can become the  crucial engine of economic growth in  India that's where the solution is for  now there's no sign of that at the local  wholesale market farmers have come in  from surrounding areas to sell the grain  and vegetables the government sets fixed  prices for certain commodities to  incentivize production but farmers here  say they're not getting those prices the  overriding view here is that farming  simply isn't viable for Bupa Patel -  there's little hope left that things  will change he sees no future in farming  all he wants is for a son to find a job  far removed from the precarious world of  Agriculture joining us for more  professor Himanshu he teaches economics  at Delhi's Jawaharlal Nehru University  professor good to see you now india's  farming crisis has been going on for  quite some time now why has it not yet  been addressed well there are a number  of reasons for this but let me just say  that agriculture is shed in the GDP is  now the lowest less and less less than  15 percent now it's not in the priority  list of the government's who are trying  to drive growths further up compared to  other countries but also because the  nature of agriculture has changed  agriculture has become much more  vulnerable because of the globalization  of the economy the nature of production  in the agricultural sector has change it  has become much  cash-based also governments over the  years have had been not investing as  much amount of money as is required for  an agriculture which has changed  dramatically in the last twenty to  thirty years so I think a combination of  all these factors and led to a situation  where agriculture continues to remain  vulnerable to external shocks weather  shocks as well as domestic internally  internal economy shocks you spoke of  money there now in terms of money both  the ruling BJP and the opposition  Congress have offered a financial  support for farmers in their manifestos  in this election season is that the  right approach you think I mean  obviously they're all both of them in  fact all political parties whether it be  the state government or the single  government are trying to treat the  symptoms when the crisis is on the  street and farmers are on the street  protesting against low prices or the  crisis in the economy this I think is  the best solution for that the  politicians could think of but remember  this does not address the real reasons  as to why the economy is in crisis and I  think the amount of money which is now  being given as a symptomatic relief of  kind of a thing if it was invested back  in the structural issues foreign issues  which basically lead to a better  productivity and better incomes to  farmers over the long run long period of  time that would have had a much better  result compared to this kind of  situation where every five years or ten  years you keep on pumping money to the  in the aggregate in economy more as a  dole more as some kind of a short-term  solution the other than as a long-term  investment in agricultural economy what  really would you say needs to be done to  tackle this farming crisis a lot of  things need to be done I mean the first  thing I think the first in the foremost  in that sense is to bring agriculture  back on the priority of economic policy  immediately in the sense agriculture may  not be contributing much to the GDP but  it still is where roughly half of the  entire workforce is and therefore for  livelihood issues agriculture is  something which is very very serious but  the nature of Agriculture has changed  and that needs to be understood it  cannot be the old-style Green Revolution  kind of a method that is what the  comments are still trying to or the  subsidy related mechanisms that the  government is still trying to pursue it  has become much more vulnerable but were  monetized much more cash based and it  has also become much more difficult to  predict what is going to happen in terms  of agricultural prices so I think you  need a kind of  series of policies to bring together all  these issues think of new ways of  dealing with the agriculture bring new  understanding of what the change is  challenges to agriculture are and then  only you can think about finding  solutions to the problems thank you very  much for explaining that to us professor  Himanshu from Delhi's Jawaharlal Nehru  University thank you
 

